    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3724.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 768,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nTo truly understand what water is, we can look at it through a few different lenses: chemistry, its unique properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Composition\nChemically, water is a molecule made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n\nThis means every single molecule of water consists of:\n*   Two hydrogen atoms\n*   One oxygen atom\n\nThese atoms are held together by strong covalent bonds. \n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and find in rivers and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. Unique and Amazing Properties\nWater has several strange and unique physical properties that make it unlike most other liquids on Earth:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it can carry chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different; when it freezes into ice, it expands and becomes *less* dense. This is why ice cubes float in a glass and why lakes freeze from the top down, allowing aquatic life to survive underneath.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why oceans help regulate the climate of the entire planet, and why our bodies don't overheat easily.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 4. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet, often called the \"Blue Planet.\"\n*   About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly by oceans.\n*   Roughly **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** in the oceans.\n*   Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and ponds.\n\n### 5. Water and Life\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n*   The human body is made up of about **60% water**. \n*   Water acts as a transporter (carrying oxygen and nutrients through the blood), a regulator (controlling body temperature through sweat), and a cleanser (helping the body flush out waste).\n*   Plants need water to perform photosynthesis—the process by which they create food and release the oxygen that humans and animals breathe.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$) with complex and miraculous properties. It shapes our geography, regulates our climate, and flows through the veins of every living thing on Earth."
